---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Quiz from '../../components/Quiz.astro';

const questions = [
  {
    question: "In the Kinesis + Lambda + S3 + Glue + Athena pattern, what breaks when someone asks for a real-time hourly metric?",
    options: [
      "Nothing, this architecture handles real-time aggregates natively",
      "The pipeline cannot compute it at all, because Lambda has no memory between invocations",
      "Athena becomes too slow to query",
      "S3 refuses to store the raw events"
    ],
    correct: 1,
    explanation: "This architecture is built for stateless ingestion. The moment a metric needs memory across events over time, there is nowhere for that memory to live."
  },
  {
    question: "Why can't a Lambda function just remember the last 10 minutes of events on its own?",
    options: [
      "Lambda functions are stateless and short-lived, with no shared memory between invocations",
      "Lambda functions are not allowed to read from Kinesis",
      "AWS deletes Lambda's memory every hour",
      "Lambda functions can only run once per day"
    ],
    correct: 0,
    explanation: "Each Lambda invocation starts and terminates with no shared memory to the next one, so it has nothing built in to remember what happened a moment ago."
  },
  {
    question: "At 10,000 events per second, what happens if a Lambda tries to maintain state by reading and writing to an external database on every event?",
    options: [
      "Nothing, external databases are built for this exact load",
      "The database gets overwhelmed by network latency, CPU load, and locking",
      "The events get automatically batched by AWS for free",
      "Lambda switches to an in-memory mode automatically"
    ],
    correct: 1,
    explanation: "Round-tripping to a database for every single event at that volume turns the database into the bottleneck, paying network and lock costs just to remember recent history."
  },
  {
    question: "How does a stateful engine like Flink avoid hitting an external database on every event?",
    options: [
      "It keeps a high-performance key-value store, typically RocksDB, embedded directly inside the processing node",
      "It stores all state in S3 instead of a database",
      "It processes every event twice to be safe",
      "It disables state entirely and recomputes from scratch each time"
    ],
    correct: 0,
    explanation: "Flink embeds RocksDB inside the processing node itself, so state updates happen locally instead of over the network."
  },
  {
    question: "During a 1-hour tumbling window in Flink, how many network calls does it make to an external reporting database?",
    options: [
      "One per incoming event",
      "Zero, until the window closes, when it makes a single consolidated write",
      "Exactly 60, one per minute",
      "It depends on how many partitions the topic has"
    ],
    correct: 1,
    explanation: "While the window is open, Flink updates its local RocksDB counter with no external calls. Only when the window closes does it write once to the reporting layer."
  },
  {
    question: "What kind of workload is serverless still a good fit for, according to this pill?",
    options: [
      "Any workload that needs aggregates over a rolling time window",
      "Stateless, short-lived, request-response work like ingestion, API backends, and file-processing triggers",
      "Long-running session tracking across days",
      "Materialized views joining two large tables"
    ],
    correct: 1,
    explanation: "Serverless remains a strong choice exactly where it was designed to work: stateless, event-driven, request-response workloads."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Quiz: State Is the Bottleneck | Streaming Pill 5"
  description="Test your understanding of why serverless struggles with state, and how stateful engines like Flink solve it with embedded local storage."
>
  <section class="pill-quiz-page">
    <div class="container">
      <a href="/blog/streaming-pill-5-state-is-the-bottleneck" class="back-link">← Back to Pill 5</a>

      <div class="pill-header">
        <div class="badge badge-series" style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Pill 5 · State Bottleneck</div>
        <h1>Quiz: Where Does the Memory Live?</h1>
        <p class="pill-lead">
          These questions test whether you understand why serverless struggles with state, and
          what a stateful engine does differently, not just that "Flink is better."
        </p>
      </div>

      <Quiz
        title="Pill 5: State Is the Real Bottleneck"
        questions={questions}
      />

      <div class="pill-footer glass-card">
        <h3>How did it go?</h3>
        <p>
          If you scored below 70%, re-read <strong>Pill 5</strong>, and picture the two bad
          options a stateless Lambda has for remembering the last 10 minutes. Which one wastes
          more, network calls or recomputation?
        </p>
        <div class="pill-footer-links">
          <a href="/blog/streaming-pill-5-state-is-the-bottleneck" class="btn btn-secondary btn-sm">Re-read Pill 5</a>
          <a href="/pills/streaming-quiz-6" class="btn btn-primary btn-sm">Next: Pill 6 Quiz — Windows</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .pill-quiz-page {
    padding: 3.5rem 0 5rem;
  }

  .back-link {
    display: inline-block;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.8rem;
    transition: color 0.2s ease;
  }
  .back-link:hover {
    color: var(--accent-blue);
  }

  .badge-series {
    background: var(--accent-blue);
    color: #ffffff;
  }

  .pill-header {
    max-width: 720px;
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }

  .pill-header h1 {
    font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 3.5vw, 2.2rem);
    margin-bottom: 0.8rem;
    line-height: 1.3;
  }

  .pill-lead {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    line-height: 1.7;
    max-width: 640px;
  }

  .pill-footer {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    padding: 2rem;
    background: var(--bg-card);
  }

  .pill-footer h3 {
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
    color: var(--text-main);
  }

  .pill-footer p {
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    margin-bottom: 1.1rem;
    max-width: 600px;
  }

  .pill-footer-links {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.65rem;
  }
</style>
